#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
provision_local_homes.py  —  private per-user folders under ONE dataset,
driven by a CSV of LOCAL TrueNAS SCALE users and groups.

Runs from ANY machine (Linux, macOS, Windows) and talks to TrueNAS over its
websocket API using an API key. Nothing is installed on the NAS, and the
credential-card PDF is written on the machine you run it from.

What it converges to, for every row in the CSV:
  - every group named in the CSV exists (local group, SMB-enabled)
  - the user exists (only with --create-users) and is a member of its CSV
    groups plus --homes-group (membership is only ever ADDED, never removed)
  - <parent>/<username>/ exists, owned by uid:gid, with an NFSv4 ACL of:
        owner@          FULL_CONTROL  (inherit)
        --admin-group   FULL_CONTROL  (inherit)   [optional break-glass]
    and NO group@ / everyone@ entries  ->  exclusive to that user
  - optional per-user quota on the parent dataset

Passphrases + 4x6 cards:
  - New users (--create-users) get a random N-word passphrase (default 3)
    drawn with `secrets` from the EFF large wordlist. No word is used twice
    in a run, so every passphrase in a batch is unique.
  - Existing users keep their password unless --reset-passwords is given.
  - Every passphrase actually set is printed to a 4x6 (landscape) PDF, one
    card per user, mode 0600. Store in a password manager, hand out, then shred.
  - Dry-run produces SAMPLE cards (watermarked, passphrases not set) so you
    can proof the layout.

With --manage-parent, the parent dataset root gets:
        owner@ (root)   FULL_CONTROL
        --homes-group   TRAVERSE only  -> can reach own folder, can't list others
        --admin-group   FULL_CONTROL

Behaviour:
  - Dry-run unless --apply. Read-only API queries still run so the plan is real.
  - Additive only: never deletes users, groups, folders, memberships or quotas.
    Folders with no matching CSV row are REPORTED as orphans, not touched.
  - Everything goes through the middleware API, so the GUI sees first-class
    objects (no shell access to the NAS is needed or used).
  - ACL changes apply to the folder itself only (non-recursive); existing
    contents are not rewritten.

Connecting:
  - TLS only (wss://). TrueNAS 25.04+ revokes an API key that is ever sent
    over plain HTTP, so this script has no non-TLS mode.
  - Uses the JSON-RPC 2.0 endpoint (/api/current, TrueNAS 25.04+) and falls
    back to the legacy endpoint (/websocket) on older releases.
  - TrueNAS ships a self-signed certificate. Either pin it:
        ./provision_local_homes.py --host nas.lan --show-fingerprint
        ... --fingerprint AB:CD:...   (compare with the NAS first, see README)
    or give the CA that signed it with --ca-cert, or install a cert your
    system already trusts.
  - The API key is read from $TRUENAS_API_KEY, from --api-key-file, or is
    prompted for. It is never accepted as a command-line argument.
  - Create the key under the user icon > API Keys. It acts as the user it
    belongs to, which needs rights to manage accounts, datasets and ACLs
    (a full admin is simplest).

CSV (header row required; '#' at start of username = comment):
    username,full_name,groups,quota
    alice,Alice Smith,staff;finance,500G
    bob,Bob Jones,staff,
    carol,,,1T

Usage (or just run it with no arguments for a guided, step-by-step setup):
    export TRUENAS_API_KEY=...            # or --api-key-file ~/.truenas-key
    ./provision_local_homes.py users.csv --host nas.lan --fingerprint AB:CD:... \
        --parent /mnt/tank/homes
    ./provision_local_homes.py users.csv --host nas.lan --fingerprint AB:CD:... \
        --parent /mnt/tank/homes --admin-group nas-admins --manage-parent
    ./provision_local_homes.py users.csv --host nas.lan --fingerprint AB:CD:... \
        --parent /mnt/tank/homes --create-users --apply
    ./provision_local_homes.py users.csv --host nas.lan --fingerprint AB:CD:... \
        --parent /mnt/tank/homes --reset-passwords --only bob \
        --share-path '\\\\nas\\homes\\{user}' --apply

Requires: Python 3.8+ (standard library only) and eff_large_wordlist.txt next
to this script (or --wordlist PATH):
    https://www.eff.org/files/2016/07/18/eff_large_wordlist.txt
The file can be used as downloaded (the dice-number column is ignored).
"""

